<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Liberal Revolution of 1871</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1871">1871</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Liberal Revolution (Guatemala)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Liberal Revolution of 1871 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> was the revolt led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miguel-garcia-granados">Miguel García Granados</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justo-rufino-barrios">Justo Rufino Barrios</a> that overthrew the conservative government of Vicente Cerna and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a> on 30 June 1871. The liberal regimes that followed expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>, confiscated church lands and promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> plantations, relying on forced indigenous labour.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/liberal-revolution-of-1871.jpg" alt="Liberal Revolution of 1871" width="320" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tomas A. Orozco · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Justo_Rufino_Barrios_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
